Work directly in the current SEREN repository and implement a focused hazard-balance pass. Do not redesign the game or rewrite unrelated systems.

SEREN is a three-lane browser arcade racer. Its road continuously accelerates from 1.00× to 3.00×, stepping upward by 0.10× every 20 seconds. Hazards are biome-specific: city uses puddles, desert uses tumbleweeds, and space uses meteors. The goal of this change is to make the road feel slightly less overcrowded with hazards as the base race speed rises, while making individual hazards slightly larger and improving collision/hitbox accuracy so contacts remain fair and visually trustworthy at high speed.

Core design goal

As the normal race-speed tier rises, hazard density should gradually decrease. Do not drastically reduce hazards and do not make the fast portion of the race empty. The player should encounter slightly fewer individual hazards per unit of road at high speed, but each hazard should have a little more visual and physical presence.

The intended balance trade is:

faster road → slightly more space between hazards → slightly larger hazards → more readable, deliberate dodges instead of increasingly cluttered hazard traffic.

This must be tied to the game’s normal speed progression, not temporary effects. Boost, Ultimate, Slow, shunts, Cole’s form-specific pace, wrecks, and other temporary speed modifiers must not dynamically change hazard density. Hazard spacing should follow G.tier / MAX_TIER, because that represents the underlying race-speed progression.

Current behavior to preserve and modify carefully

The current speed tuning in js/data.js is:

BASE_SPEED = 420

SPEED_SECONDS = 20

MULT_STEP = 0.10

MAX_MULT = 3.00

MAX_TIER = 20

The current hazard spawning in js/race.js accumulates travelled road distance into G.trapGap and spawns when it reaches G.nextTrap. After a spawn, the next gap is currently:

rand(430, 900)

This is fixed regardless of speed tier.

Keep the existing distance-based spawning architecture. Do not convert traps to a timer-based spawn system. Distance-based spawning is appropriate for SEREN and should remain the source of truth.

Instead, make the random road-distance gap gradually larger as G.tier approaches MAX_TIER.

Hazard-density scaling

At tier 0 / 1.00× speed, preserve today’s hazard spacing exactly or effectively exactly: approximately 430–900 road pixels.

At maximum base progression / 3.00×, increase the hazard spacing range by approximately 18%.

Use a simple monotonic interpolation based on normalized tier progress:

tierProgress = clamp(G.tier / MAX_TIER, 0, 1)

spacingScale = lerp(1.00, 1.18, tierProgress)

Then generate subsequent gaps from the existing range multiplied by that scale.

The resulting approximate targets should be:

1.00×: 430–900
1.50×: 449–941
2.00×: 469–981
2.50×: 488–1022
3.00×: 507–1062

The exact integer rounding is unimportant. The progression and gameplay feel are what matter.

This makes maximum-speed road-distance density roughly 15% lower than at the start rather than aggressively reducing hazard frequency.

Do not scale with G.speed, because G.speed includes transient gameplay effects and throttle interpolation. The road should not suddenly create fewer hazards because somebody activated an Ultimate or more hazards because the player was slowed.

Keep the existing seam rule: spawnTrap() must still refuse to spawn while G.seam !== null.

Keep custom races authoritative: ruleOn("traps") must continue to disable hazards entirely.

Keep the initial G.nextTrap = 620 behavior unless there is a strong technical reason to change it. The race starts at the minimum tier anyway, so the first hazard should remain familiar.

Prefer named tuning values rather than introducing unexplained new magic numbers. If the existing 430 and 900 values are promoted into constants, put them with the other gameplay tuning in js/data.js and update docs/TUNING.md.

A reasonable naming scheme would be conceptually equivalent to:

TRAP_GAP_MIN
TRAP_GAP_MAX
TRAP_GAP_MAX_SCALE

Do not duplicate the formula in multiple files. There should be one authoritative helper or calculation for the next hazard gap.

Make hazards slightly larger

Increase the physical hazard size only modestly. The intention is around 5–8%, not a dramatic redesign.

For puddles, increase the main rx / ry footprint by approximately 8%. Preserve the existing randomized aspect ratio and irregular seeded perimeter. The puddle should still look organically different each time.

For tumbleweeds, increase the main r by approximately 8%.

For meteors, increase both the warning/blast footprint and falling rock itself modestly, approximately 5–8%, but preserve the existing lane-safety philosophy. A meteor centered in one lane must not become so large that simply moving cleanly into a neighboring lane becomes unreliable.

Do not make all hazard artwork enormous merely because peripheral effects exist. Shadows, glows, water droplets, meteor fire trails, explosion animation, and loose tumbleweed twig tips are presentation and should not automatically become damaging geometry.

The size increase must apply coherently to both visuals and gameplay geometry. Do not enlarge the drawing while leaving the actual contact area at the old dimensions, and do not invisibly enlarge a hitbox farther than the hazard appears to occupy.

Hitbox accuracy — preserve SEREN’s existing strengths

SEREN already has a strong collision system. Do not replace it with generic axis-aligned rectangles.

Every playable vehicle has a traced hitShape in logical car units. carHit() gets the current model through racerModel(who) and its dimensions through racerDims(who), rotates the polygon with the car, and returns the resulting world-space points.

Preserve that system exactly.

Do not simplify vehicle hitboxes.

Do not use sprite rectangular bounds as collision geometry.

Do not add invisible padding to cars.

Alternate forms must continue switching their visual model and collision hull together.

The generic CAR_HIT_RECT should remain only a fallback, as it is now.

Centralize hazard collision geometry

Improve the hazard side of collision handling so the geometry used by gameplay, perfect-dodge detection, bots, players, and rendering cannot quietly diverge.

Create or refine shared hazard-geometry helpers where appropriate rather than maintaining slightly different radius calculations in several places.

The desired conceptual architecture is:

car geometry comes from carHit().

puddle geometry comes from the same irregular path used to draw the main puddle.

tumbleweed geometry comes from one defined solid-body envelope.

meteor blast geometry comes from the warning/blast circle.

falling meteor-rock geometry comes from one shared rock-radius calculation.

Gameplay and perfect-dodge prediction should ask those same definitions.

Do not create separate “player hitbox” and “bot hitbox” versions.

Puddle precision

The puddle implementation is already particularly good.

puddlePoints() generates the seeded irregular outline.

drawPuddle() draws that outline through puddlePath().

puddleHits() subdivides the same quadratic perimeter and performs polygon overlap against the car hull.

Keep this architecture.

When increasing puddle size, feed the enlarged rx and ry into this same path so visual size and collision geometry remain synchronized automatically.

The small detached water droplets drawn outside the primary puddle should stay cosmetic. Do not make them collision geometry.

Do not replace the irregular puddle hitbox with an ellipse or circle.

Tumbleweed precision

The current tumbleweed visually consists of a procedural rotating bundle, while gameplay uses a circular proximity test based on o.r.

Make the tumbleweed’s collision envelope explicit and centralized.

The damaging body should correspond to the dense visible tumbleweed mass, not every isolated twig end and not an oversized invisible circle.

A small conservative inset relative to the outermost twig art is acceptable and desirable because individual sticks extending beyond the dense ball are visually porous. What matters is consistency: define the effective collision radius once and use it everywhere that needs to reason about a tumbleweed, including live collisions and perfect-dodge prediction.

If the existing effective factor around sqrt(0.86) remains visually correct after the size change, it may be retained, but give the concept a shared helper or named tuning value so updateTraps(), rival hazard handling, and perfect-dodge prediction cannot diverge.

Do not make contact easier simply by expanding the logical collision factor on top of the requested 8% overall hazard-size increase unless playtesting clearly shows the visible body requires it.

Meteor precision

The meteor has two distinct collision concepts and they must remain distinct.

The first is the ground warning/blast radius o.r.

The red warning ring shown on the road represents the eventual blast area. When the meteor detonates, collision should correspond precisely to that circle. Preserve the current principle that the ring and damaging blast are the same physical footprint.

The second is the falling rock itself, represented by o.mr.

Currently the renderer derives a visible rock radius using the fall-progress scale:

the visible rock uses o.mr * sc

while roof collision uses o.mr directly.

Remove that potential visual/physical disagreement.

Introduce a single helper conceptually equivalent to meteorRockRadius(o) that returns the current visible solid-rock radius based on o.mr, o.fall, and rockLead(o).

Use the same helper in both drawMeteor() and the falling-rock collision check.

Do not include the rock’s glow or flame trail in the collision radius.

Do not include its ground shadow.

Only the visible solid rock itself should make direct falling-rock contact.

The blast circle after impact remains a separate collision event using o.r.

rockAlt() must remain the single authority for meteor altitude.

Do not create another altitude formula.

Prevent high-speed tunneling

This change matters because the road reaches 3.00× and hazards are being made slightly larger.

Collision accuracy should not depend on a hazard and a car happening to overlap at the exact end of a rendered frame.

SEREN already uses sweptY() for parts of player hazard contact and uses very fine substeps in perfect-dodge prediction, capped around one logical pixel / 1/240 second.

Preserve those strengths and make actual hazard contact robust for both Player 1 and rivals.

Do not simply increase hitboxes to hide tunneling.

Where relative motion during one frame is large enough to jump across a contact boundary, test the swept motion between the previous and current positions.

Use a cheap broad phase first so this does not become unnecessarily expensive. There are only seven racers and relatively few live hazards, so precise narrow-phase tests near a possible contact are reasonable.

For irregular puddles, the actual puddle polygon remains authoritative.

For tumbleweeds and meteor circles, use the shared collision radii.

For cars, use their existing polygon hulls.

If substeps are required, use deterministic bounded substeps comparable to the perfect-dodge system rather than FPS-dependent arbitrary sampling.

The same physical encounter should resolve the same way at 30 FPS, 60 FPS, 120 FPS, or after the existing frame-delta clamp.

Avoid double hits. Existing o.hit, o.nm, per-racer bits, shield behavior, perfect-dodge bookkeeping, and hazard destruction/removal semantics must continue working.

Player/rival parity

SEREN’s architecture deliberately makes bots use the player’s mechanics.

Preserve this invariant.

The player-side hazard logic in js/mechanics.js and rival-side hazard handling in js/race.js currently share concepts such as:

carHit()
nearestOnCar()
puddleHits()
clearsSolidHazards()
markPassed()

Do not introduce new geometry that only one side uses.

If collision geometry is centralized, both paths should consume it.

A bot, local human, or Player 1 driving the same model through the same hazard should be judged against the same physical shape.

Difficulty settings must not alter collision sizes.

Preserve all existing hazard consequences

This is a density/size/hitbox pass, not a hazard-rules redesign.

City puddles must still apply the existing blind/Obscured behavior and shield rules.

Tumbleweeds must still apply the existing Slow behavior and destruction behavior.

Meteors must still use their current timed fall, direct falling-rock interaction, ground detonation, wreck behavior, and Saffron interception.

clearsSolidHazards() behavior must remain intact.

Ultimate-specific hazard interactions must remain intact.

Rhosyn’s Aero-Glow absence from the shared road must remain intact.

Saffron’s airborne behavior and meteor interception must remain intact.

Respawn invulnerability, finish protection, shield durability, perfect-dodge rewards, trap penalties, and all car-specific powers must remain unchanged except where improved geometry makes contact physically more accurate.

Do not change BLIND_TIME, SLOW_TIME, DEAD_TIME, INVULNERABLE_TIME, ultimate charge, shield counts, damage consequences, race speed, or boost tuning as part of this task.

Rendering

Keep SEREN’s current Canvas 2D visual style.

Hazards should look like the same hazards they are now, merely a little larger.

Do not redesign puddles.

Do not redesign tumbleweeds.

Do not redesign meteors.

Do not make warning graphics disproportionately larger than their actual danger.

The warning ring of a meteor should remain an honest preview of the eventual blast radius.

Keep culling correct after the size increases. Review trapReach() and drawTraps() so a larger hazard is not clipped or culled slightly too early, especially in split-screen.

Preserve split-screen correctness: W remains one viewport, while VW_TOP / VW_BOT cover the shared world required by every viewport.

Files likely involved

The main implementation should remain localized.

js/race.js — hazard gap progression and rival hazard-contact integration.

js/mechanics.js — spawnTrap(), hazard geometry, collision helpers, player trap collision, perfect-dodge geometry, meteor/tumbleweed precision.

js/render.js — use shared meteor rock geometry and confirm hazard drawing/culling follows enlarged dimensions.

js/data.js — add named tuning constants only if useful for the new spacing/size parameters.

docs/TUNING.md — document the updated spacing behavior, new tuning constants, approximate max-tier density change, and hazard size policy.

Only touch docs/ARCHITECTURE.md if the collision authority materially changes enough that its description would otherwise become inaccurate.

Do not modify unrelated UI, menu, AI judgement, car abilities, item balance, race length, speed progression, or artwork.

Code-quality expectations

Follow the repository’s existing classic-script/global-scope architecture.

Do not introduce modules, imports, a bundler, npm dependencies, or external libraries.

All new top-level names must be unique across the entire script set.

Avoid browser-global names.

Keep one authority per gameplay question.

Prefer short, descriptive helpers over repeated formulas.

Comments should explain design invariants, not restate obvious JavaScript.

Do not silently duplicate a hitbox formula between mechanics.js, race.js, and render.js.

Acceptance criteria

1. A race at tier 0 retains approximately the current 430–900 hazard-gap range.
2. Hazard spacing grows smoothly/monotonically with G.tier, reaching about 1.18× the original gap at MAX_TIER.
3. Temporary Boost, Ultimate, Slow, wrecking, shunts, or individual vehicle pace do not affect the hazard-spacing scale.
4. At 3.00× base progression, road-distance hazard density is modestly lower rather than dramatically lower.
5. Puddles and tumbleweeds are approximately 8% larger; meteors are only modestly larger and retain a reliable safe neighboring-lane escape.
6. Puddle collision continues matching the irregular main puddle outline rather than an ellipse.
7. Detached puddle droplets remain cosmetic.
8. Car collision continues using each model’s traced rotated polygon.
9. Tumbleweed collision has one shared, documented solid-body radius used consistently by gameplay and dodge prediction.
10. The falling meteor’s visible solid-rock radius and direct-contact radius come from the same calculation.
11. Meteor glow, fire trail, shadow, and explosion presentation do not become direct-contact hitboxes.
12. The meteor warning circle accurately previews its ground blast radius.
13. High-speed encounters cannot routinely tunnel through a hazard merely because no final-frame overlap occurred.
14. Player 1, bots, and local-human rivals use identical hazard geometry.
15. Existing o.hit, per-racer masks, perfect-dodge state, shields, immunity, Ultimates, Saffron interception, Aero-Glow, and finishing protections continue behaving correctly.
16. No unrelated gameplay constants are changed.
17. Split-screen hazard drawing/culling remains correct.
18. node tools/check.mjs passes with no new errors.
19. Test at 1.00×, roughly 2.00×, and 3.00× and verify visually that the intended progression is: same density at the beginning, modestly more breathing room later, slightly larger hazards throughout.
20. Specifically test edge grazes against every vehicle size/form, including narrow cars, larger race-scaled cars, Cole’s bike, Neela’s alternate form, and Saffron’s special states, so collision follows the actual current model rather than a generic rectangle.

The finished result should feel subtle. A player familiar with SEREN should not think hazards were removed or redesigned. They should notice that the extremely fast part of a race is a little less cluttered, individual hazards are easier to read because they have slightly more presence, and near-edge collisions feel trustworthy because what visually touches the car is what the game actually evaluates.
